      "sequence_position": 2,
      "problem": "Suppose we wish to construct a proof by contrapositive of the following statement:\n\nLet $n$ be an integer. Then $2{n}^{2}-7n+1$ is odd only if $n$ is even.\n\nA proof schema is outlined below. Fill in the missing entries.\n\nThe contrapositive form of the statement is as follows:\n\n$$\n{{field-1}}\\,⇒\\,{{field-2}}\n$$\n\nIf $n$ is {{field-3}}, then $n=$ {{field-4}}, where $a$ is {{field-5}}.\n\nSubstituting {{field-6}} into the expression $2{n}^{2}-7n+1,$ we can show that $2{n}^{2}-7n+1=$ {{field-7}}, where $b$ is {{field-8}}.\n\nTherefore, since $2{n}^{2}-7n+1$ {{field-9}} a multiple of $2,$ we conclude that $2{n}^{2}-7n+1$ is {{field-10}}.",
      "worked_solution": "Recall the following:\n\n- If an integer $n$ is even, then there exists an integer $p$ such that $n=2p.$\n\n- If an integer $n$ is odd, then there exists an integer $q$ such that $n=2q+1.$\n\nIn this case, we need to prove the following:\n\n$$\n2{n}^{2}-7n+1\\text{ is odd}\\,⇒\\,n\\text{ is even}\n$$\n\nTo prove $P⇒Q,$ it suffices to prove the contrapositive $¬Q⇒¬P.$\n\nThe contrapositive form of the given statement is\n\n$$\nn\\text{ is not even}\\,⇒\\,2{n}^{2}-7n+1\\text{ is not odd}\n$$\n\nwhich is equivalent to\n\n$$\nn\\text{ is odd}\\,⇒\\,2{n}^{2}-7n+1\\text{ is even}.\n$$\n\nWe start by using the fact that $n$ is odd.\n\nIf $n$ is $\\text{odd},$ then\n$n=2a+1,$\nwhere $a$ is $\\text{an integer}.$\n\nThe idea is to substitute $n=2a+1$ into the expression $2{n}^{2}-7n+1$ and show it is even by writing it as a multiple of $2.$\n\nSubstituting $n=2a+1$ into the expression $2{n}^{2}-7n+1,$ we can show that $2{n}^{2}-7n+1=2b,$ where $b$ is $\\text{an integer}.$\n\nOnce we've done this, the proof is complete, and we can state our conclusion.\n\nTherefore, since $2{n}^{2}-7n+1$ $\\text{is}$ a multiple of $2,$ we conclude that $2{n}^{2}-7n+1$ is $\\text{even}.$",
